        {
          "id": "ch14_ex11_q3a",
          "number": "3(a)",
          "category": null,
          "questionText": "Solve $2\\tan(2x) = 2$ for $x \\in [-\\pi, \\pi]$.",
          "questionFigures": [],
          "solution": {
            "formula": "$\\tan u = 1 \\iff u = \\frac{\\pi}{4} + k\\pi$, $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ (tangent has period $\\pi$).",
            "steps": [
              "Given: $2\\tan(2x) = 2$, $x \\in [-\\pi, \\pi]$. Goal: all solutions.",
              "$\\tan(2x) = 1$. Let $u = 2x$, with $u \\in [-2\\pi, 2\\pi]$.",
              "$u = \\frac{\\pi}{4} + k\\pi$: $u = -\\frac{7\\pi}{4}, -\\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{5\\pi}{4}$.",
              "$x = \\frac{u}{2}$: $x = -\\frac{7\\pi}{8}, -\\frac{3\\pi}{8}, \\frac{\\pi}{8}, \\frac{5\\pi}{8}$.",
              "Check: $x = \\frac{5\\pi}{8}$ gives $2\\tan\\frac{5\\pi}{4} = 2(1) = 2$. Correct. Four solutions is expected, since the period $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ fits four times into $[-\\pi, \\pi]$."
            ],
            "finalAnswer": "$x = -\\frac{7\\pi}{8}, -\\frac{3\\pi}{8}, \\frac{\\pi}{8}, \\frac{5\\pi}{8}$",
            "solutionFigures": [],
            "tip": "For tangent, add and subtract multiples of $\\pi$, not $2\\pi$."
          }
        },
